---
/*
 * Drivers — v1 receipts page ported (design-spec-v2 §6). Owner: w5-receipts-chrome.
 * Body markup CONTENT-IDENTICAL to site/drivers.html between the shared chrome.
 * Chrome swapped to v2 (global.css + SiteHeader/SiteFooter). Data path /data/ via common.js.
 * No Google Fonts. Inline enhancement script kept as-is (is:inline).
 */
import "../styles/global.css";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Drivers &middot; US Wildfires</title>
<meta name="description" content="Drought moves fire in most states but not all: state-by-state drought-fire correlation, the fuel-limited exception, and the mechanism literature.">
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg">
</head>
<body>
<SiteHeader active="drivers" />

<main class="page page-drivers">

  <section class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">The explainer</p>
    <h1>What moves the fire: drought, fuel, and people</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The trend and map pages show <em>that</em> fire years have grown. This page is about
      <em>why</em>. Drought is the first suspect, and in most states it is a strong one &mdash;
      but not everywhere, and the exceptions are as informative as the rule. These are the
      ingredients; the <a href="/model.html">model page</a> prices them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== 1. DROUGHT -> FIRE ===================== -->
  <section class="drivers-block">
    <h2>1. Drought moves fire &mdash; in most states</h2>
    <p>
      For each state we ranked its years by growing-season drought and by how much it burned,
      then measured how tightly the two rankings track. A strong negative value means the dry
      years are the big-fire years.
    </p>
    <p class="spec-quote mono">
      Spearman rank correlation: state May&ndash;Oct mean PDSI vs log<sub>10</sub> annual FOD acres,
      1992&ndash;2020; states with &ge;25 years and &gt;50k total acres. Negative = drier &rarr; more burned.
      <!-- STAT: trend_stats.fire_climate_correlation.spec -->
    </p>
    <p>
      Colorado is the headline: <span class="mono">&rho; = &minus;0.72</span> &mdash; its driest
      growing seasons are almost perfectly its worst fire years.
      <!-- STAT: trend_stats.fire_climate_correlation.strongest_negative_10[0] (CO -0.724) -->
      Across all <span class="mono">38</span> qualifying states the median correlation is
      <span class="mono">&minus;0.45</span>, and <span class="mono">15</span> states clear the
      strong-negative bar.
      <!-- STAT: trend_stats.fire_climate_correlation.n_states (38); .median_r (-0.446); .n_strong_negative (15) -->
      The signal is real and it is broad &mdash; drier growing season, more acres burned.
    </p>

    <div id="drought-tilemap" class="tilemap-canvas" role="img"
         aria-label="US state tile-grid map. Each state tile is shaded by its Spearman correlation between growing-season drought and log annual acres burned, on a diverging cool-to-ember scale. Ember tiles (strong negative, drier means more fire) include Colorado at minus 0.72; cool tiles (weak or positive) include Nevada at plus 0.13.">
      <p class="data-fallback">
        Interactive tile-map unavailable &mdash; the top-10 correlation table below carries the
        strongest state values.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="map-note mono">
      Diverging scale: ember = strong negative (drier&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;more fire) &middot;
      cool = weak or positive. 50 states shown; DC excluded (not a state).
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== 2. THE WRINKLE ===================== -->
  <section id="wrinkle" class="drivers-block wrinkle">
    <h2>2. The wrinkle: fuel-limited deserts break the rule</h2>
    <p>
      If drought drove fire everywhere, the drought&ndash;fire correlation would be strongly
      negative in every state. It is not. Two of the driest states in the country sit at the
      bottom of the list:
    </p>
    <ul class="wrinkle-list">
      <li><strong>Arizona</strong>: <span class="mono">&rho; = &minus;0.18</span> (not significant, p&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.36)
        <!-- STAT: trend_stats.fire_climate_correlation.west_11 (AZ -0.177, p 0.3574) --></li>
      <li><strong>Nevada</strong>: <span class="mono">&rho; = +0.13</span> &mdash; the wrong sign
        entirely (p&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.50)
        <!-- STAT: trend_stats.fire_climate_correlation.west_11 (NV +0.13, p 0.5013) --></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The rest of the interior West behaves as expected &mdash; Colorado
      <span class="mono">&minus;0.72</span>, New Mexico <span class="mono">&minus;0.52</span>,
      California <span class="mono">&minus;0.51</span>, Wyoming <span class="mono">&minus;0.46</span>
      &mdash; with the correlation weakening as you move into the drier, sparser Great Basin
      (Idaho <span class="mono">&minus;0.33</span>, Utah <span class="mono">&minus;0.28</span>)
      until it inverts in Arizona and Nevada.
      <!-- STAT: trend_stats.fire_climate_correlation.west_11 (CO,NM,CA,WY,ID,UT values) -->
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        The likely reason is that these deserts are <strong>fuel-limited, not
        flammability-limited</strong>. In a Colorado forest, the fuel is always there; what
        varies year to year is whether it is dry enough to burn, so drought and fire move together.
        In the Nevada desert, the fuel &mdash; fine grasses between the shrubs &mdash; is <em>not</em>
        always there. A wet year grows the grass; the <em>following</em> dry year burns it. Fire
        chases the wet year, not the dry one, which pushes the same-year drought&ndash;fire
        correlation toward zero or positive.
      </p>
      <p class="observed-note">
        This flammability-limited vs. fuel-limited split is <strong>observed in this data</strong>
        (the AZ/NV sign reversal above) and is <strong>consistent with the fuel-limitation
        mechanism described in the fire-ecology literature</strong>. We did not model the
        one-year lag here; the pattern is presented as an observed exception with a
        literature-consistent explanation, not a fitted result.
        <!-- UNVERIFIED-CITE: fuel-limited mechanism -->
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      This is exactly where the <a href="/model.html">model page</a> will show Nevada&rsquo;s
      out-of-sample skill going <em>negative</em>: a same-year drought model cannot predict a
      state where fire follows last year&rsquo;s wet growth. The wrinkle here is the failure mode there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== 3. MECHANISM ANCHORS ===================== -->
  <section class="drivers-block anchors">
    <h2>3. Why drought turns into fire &mdash; the mechanism</h2>
    <p>
      The correlations above are ours. Why the relationship exists &mdash; and how much of it is
      human-driven &mdash; rests on the published attribution literature. Three anchors carry the
      weight, quoted verbatim.
    </p>

    <figure class="anchor-quote">
      <blockquote>
        &ldquo;Anthropogenic climate change accounted for &sim;55% of observed increases in fuel
        aridity from 1979 to 2015 across western US forests&hellip; human-caused climate change
        contributed to an additional 4.2 million ha of forest fire area during 1984&ndash;2015,
        nearly doubling the forest fire area expected in its absence.&rdquo;
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption>
        &mdash; Abatzoglou &amp; Williams 2016 <a href="#ref-abatzoglou2016" class="cite">[abatzoglou2016]</a>.
        <span class="anchor-gloss">Drought is the pathway: warming dries the fuel, and roughly
        half of that drying is attributable to human-caused climate change.</span>
        <!-- STAT: research/r3-provenance-lit.md B3 (abatzoglou2016 verbatim ~55% fuel aridity) -->
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="anchor-quote">
      <blockquote>
        &ldquo;Prior work shows western US forest wildfire activity increased abruptly in the
        mid-1980s&hellip; Wildfire activity appears strongly associated with warming and earlier
        spring snowmelt.&rdquo;
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption>
        &mdash; Westerling 2016 <a href="#ref-westerling2016" class="cite">[westerling2016]</a>.
        <span class="anchor-gloss">Not just how dry, but how long: earlier snowmelt lengthens the
        fire season, giving drought more of the calendar to work in.</span>
        <!-- STAT: research/r3-provenance-lit.md B4 (westerling2016 verbatim season/snowmelt) -->
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="anchor-quote">
      <blockquote>
        &ldquo;Humans have vastly expanded the spatial and seasonal &lsquo;fire niche&rsquo; in the
        coterminous United States, accounting for 84% of all wildfires and 44% of total area
        burned&hellip; the human-caused fire season was three times longer than the lightning-caused
        fire season and added an average of 40,000 wildfires per year across the United States.&rdquo;
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption>
        &mdash; Balch et al. 2017 <a href="#ref-balch2017" class="cite">[balch2017]</a>.
        <span class="anchor-gloss">Drought sets the stage; people supply most of the ignitions.</span>
        <!-- STAT: research/r3-provenance-lit.md B6 (balch2017 verbatim 84% / 44%) -->
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reconcile-note">
      <p>
        <strong>84% vs. 77% &mdash; a denominator note.</strong> Balch et al. report
        <span class="mono">84%</span> of wildfires as human-caused; elsewhere on this site we cite
        <span class="mono">77%</span> from our own extract. Both are correct under their own
        denominators. Balch computes the human share <em>among cause-attributed fires</em> (an
        earlier 1992&ndash;2012 edition); our <span class="mono">77%</span> is the human share of
        <em>all records</em>, including the 8% whose cause is &ldquo;Missing data / not specified /
        undetermined.&rdquo; Fold that 8% out of the denominator and the two figures converge.
        These are not contradictory numbers &mdash; they are the same fires counted against
        different bases.
        <!-- STAT: research/r3-provenance-lit.md B6 (denominator reconciliation: 84% cause-known vs 77% all-records) -->
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="caution">
      A note on certainty: attribution is probabilistic and partial. &ldquo;~55% of the fuel-aridity
      increase&rdquo; and &ldquo;nearly doubling forest fire area&rdquo; are statements about the
      aggregate, not about any single fire. Nothing on this page says climate change caused a
      particular fire; the correlations are correlational, and the attribution figures are quoted
      as their authors stated them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== 4. BRIDGE ===================== -->
  <section class="drivers-block bridge">
    <h2>4. The ingredients of prediction</h2>
    <p>
      Put the three together and you have the recipe the record keeps writing: drought dries the
      fuel (Abatzoglou &amp; Williams), a longer, warmer season gives it more room (Westerling),
      and people supply most of the ignitions (Balch) &mdash; except where the fuel itself is the
      limiting ingredient, as in the desert states above. These are the ingredients. The
      <a href="/model.html">model page</a> takes drought, temperature, precipitation, and
      seasonality and asks the harder question: <strong>how much of a fire year can you actually
      predict from them, and where does that prediction break?</strong> The answer &mdash; a modest
      but real skill over climatology, strong in Colorado and California, negative in Nevada &mdash;
      is the drivers story, priced.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== NO-JS TABLE ===================== -->
  <noscript>
  <section class="static-table">
    <h2>Strongest drought&ndash;fire correlations (top 10 states)</h2>
    <p class="table-note">
      State Spearman &rho; between May&ndash;Oct mean PDSI and log<sub>10</sub> annual FOD acres,
      1992&ndash;2020. More negative = drier years are bigger-fire years.
    </p>
    <table class="data-table">
      <caption>Top-10 negative drought&ndash;fire correlations by state</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">Spearman &rho;</th><th scope="col">p</th><th scope="col">Years</th></tr>
      </thead>
